INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - COURTROOM 4 - NIGHT

Power's out. Emergency lights buzz amber. The gallery is empty. Four people, one table.

LUCIA FERRANTE, 40s, defense counsel, sleeves rolled. BENEDICT HAYES, 50s, prosecutor, tie loosened. JONAH KEPLER, 22, defendant, hoodie, handcuffed to the chair. Rain on the windows.

HAYES
(to the room)
Anyone have a flashlight?

FERRANTE
Phone. Dying.

JONAH
I could've told you the wiring in this place is garbage.

FERRANTE
Jonah. Quiet.

INTERCOM (O.S.)
(crackling)
Courtroom Four, this is Security. Generator's down. Stay seated. Repeat. Stay seated.

HAYES
Copy.

FERRANTE
(muttering)
Of course it dies on closing argument.

Hayes stands. Paces. Stops at the exhibit table.

HAYES
Ms. Ferrante, the knife.

FERRANTE
What about it?

HAYES
It's gone.

Ferrante's head snaps up. Jonah's chair scrapes.

FERRANTE
Gone how?

HAYES
Evidence bag's open. Tag's still there.

JONAH
I didn't touch anything.

FERRANTE
Jonah, shut up.

HAYES
(muttering)
Chain of custody. Dear God.

Hayes lunges for the bag. Ferrante gets there first. They both freeze. Under the tag, taped to the plastic, is a note. She reads it.

FERRANTE
(low)
"Found in my locker. Sorry. -- Deputy Marsh."

Hayes takes the note. Reads it. Sits down hard.

JONAH
So, what, I'm free?

HAYES
No.

FERRANTE
Not yet.

JONAH
But that's good, right?

FERRANTE
Jonah.

JONAH
Okay. Quiet.

The lights stutter back on. The room is bright and ordinary. Hayes sets the note on the table between them, and leaves his hand flat on top of it.

INTERCOM (O.S.)
(crackling)
Power's restored. Carry on.

Nobody moves.
